// TestFramework.Perf
// This library exists to give the coverage corpus's timing scripts the operator they call. The engine
// has the node (SystemBenchmarkExecuteNode in src/engine/DAE/Runtime.Instructions/SystemNodes.cs: it runs
// a script and returns the elapsed time), but no shipped D4 file binds a name to it, so Timings.d4 could
// not resolve BenchmarkExecute and failed. It is declared here rather than in TestFramework.Coverage.Base
// because that helper library is registered for every coverage script, so a failure in it would fail all
// of them; this library is registered only by the manifest entries that ask for it (test-perf-timing-scripts).

// The tags are not optional, and they are the one thing here that can silently falsify a measurement.
// A create operator defaults IsLiteral, IsFunctional, IsDeterministic and IsRepeatable to true
// (Schema.Operator.InternalInitialize). IsLiteral means compile-time evaluable, and every call in
// Timings.d4 passes a string literal, so a tagless declaration invites the compiler to run the benchmark
// while compiling it; deterministic and repeatable invite the plan cache and common-subexpression
// elimination to hand back one measurement for several calls. This is the tag set the engine's own
// Execute operator carries in src/engine/DAE/Schema/SystemCatalog.d4.
create operator BenchmarkExecute(const AString : String) : TimeSpan
	class "System.SystemBenchmarkExecuteNode"
	static tags { DAE.IsLiteral = "False", DAE.IsRemotable = "False", DAE.IsFunctional = "False", DAE.IsDeterministic = "False", DAE.IsRepeatable = "False" };

// Timings inserts 10,000 rows into a table on the library's default device, and the engine's in-memory
// System.Temp device refuses the 1,001st row (Engine.TempDeviceMaxRowCount; error 114113). The script
// predates 3.1 and its own recorded numbers are for 10,000 rows, so it ran with the cap lifted: the old
// hand-wired fixture registered TestFramework.Coverage.Devices, whose Register.d4 lifts it. Coverage
// scripts are the record of correct behaviour and are never edited, so the cap is lifted here instead -
// once, for the two consumers that register this library (the Timings manifest entry and PerfBaselineTest)
// and for nothing else. LongAggregates.d4 does the same thing inside its own script.
alter device System.Temp alter class { alter "MaxRowCount" = "-1" };
